{
	"$schema": "./node_modules/fallow/schema.json",
	// Process entry points, not import roots. `scripts/lint-edited.mjs` is the third
	// kind: nothing imports it and no npm script runs it — Claude Code spawns it per edit,
	// named in `.claude/settings.json`. Declared here rather than given a contrived npm
	// script, and `tests/build/lint-edited.test.ts` is what checks the naming still resolves.
	// `scripts/harness-shot.mjs` is the same shape from the other direction: an npm script
	// (`harness-shot`) does run it, but fallow's import graph starts from `entry` and this
	// file imports nothing under `src/` or `tests/` — nothing else in the graph reaches it
	// either, so it needs the same declaration or fallow reports it dead.
	// `scripts/concept-shots.mjs` is the same case as `harness-shot.mjs` and needs the same
	// line, and so is `scripts/background-fixture.mjs` — an npm script runs it
	// (`background-fixture`) to redraw the PNG the manual smoke test uses as a plan
	// background, and it imports nothing under `src/` or `tests/`. `scripts/chromium.mjs` deliberately does NOT: both of them import it, so fallow
	// reaches it through the graph — and it having to be reachable is the point of it being
	// one file rather than a copy in each.
	// The eight `*.test-d.ts` files are a fourth kind, next to `scripts/lint-edited.mjs` above:
	// nothing imports them and no npm script runs them either — `vue-tsc -noEmit`
	// type-checks them because `tsconfig.json`'s `include` names each one directly, and
	// compiling is the whole point. `type-safety.test-d.ts` holds the screen/world brand
	// separation and a `SelectionStore` assignability check; `diagnostics.test-d.ts` holds
	// slice 11's no-project-content guarantee, which is a claim about `DiagnosticsLedger.record`'s
	// PARAMETERS and so has no runtime form at all; `fixtureVault.test-d.ts` holds slice 12's
	// `VaultSurface` widening, proved in both directions; `errorSurfacePolicy.test-d.ts` holds
	// slice 17's ACCESS rule — `ErrorSurface` carries a `unique symbol` its own module never
	// exports, so a hand-built surface cannot reach the toast door and the only way to hold one
	// is to have asked `surfaceFor`; `exceptionMapper.test-d.ts` holds the obligation that every
	// mapper stamp the fault it mints, which is a claim about mappers NOT YET WRITTEN and so has
	// no runtime form either — the rule in its earlier, remembered form was kept at one of its
	// two sites and missed the one every guarded command goes through; `currency.test-d.ts` holds
	// the branded `Currency`'s ACCESS rule — a `unique symbol` this module never exports, so the
	// only way to hold one is to have gone through `parseCurrency` or `currencyOf`;
	// `costPipelineInput.test-d.ts` holds `CostPipelineInput.expectedCurrency` being REQUIRED — a
	// claim only the compiler can hold, since a runtime test cannot distinguish "omitted" from
	// "omitted and defaulted"; `assetLibraryContext.test-d.ts` (Task 11 review) holds
	// `AssetLibraryContext.assetId`/`.expanded` being `DeepReadonly<Ref<T>>` rather than a bare
	// `Ref<T>` — a claim about what the injected TYPE refuses a consumer, which is identical at
	// runtime either way (both are the same writable ref underneath, assigned with NO cast at
	// the one `provide()` call — a writable `Ref<T>` is structurally assignable into a
	// `DeepReadonly<Ref<T>>`-typed slot) and so has no runtime form. In all eight, an
	// unsatisfied `@ts-expect-error` or assignment failure is itself a compile error, which is the
	// mechanism. Named one at a time rather than
	// globbed `tests/**/*.test-d.ts`, for the reason `dynamicallyLoaded`'s own comment gives:
	// a glob absorbs the next file and tells nobody — and here it would absorb one whose
	// tsconfig `include` entry had been forgotten, leaving a file that is neither compiled
	// nor reported.
	// `src/prototypes/**/*.vue` is a fifth kind: the harness index reaches every one of them
	// through `import.meta.glob`, which is a VITE feature resolved at build time and invisible
	// to a static import graph — so fallow sees a tree nothing imports and reports every file
	// dead. Declared as entries rather than as `dynamicallyLoaded` because a prototype is a
	// starting point of the harness's graph and fallow has to walk outward from it — and
	// walking outward is load-bearing now rather than vacuous: a prototype CAN import, and
	// `AssetLibrary.vue` imports two sibling mocks and a fixture that imports
	// `src/core/geometry/Point`. This paragraph used to end "NOT because a prototype imports
	// anything: it cannot — `vue/no-restricted-block` bans `<script>` in that tree", which was
	// true when it was written and stopped being true when a `<script setup>` became legal here.
	// `src/prototypes/README.md` records that same claim going stale in three other places; this
	// was the fourth, and it survived them because nothing in any gate reads a comment.
	// `tests/fixtures/promotion/ZoneSummary.promoted.vue` is a sixth kind: Task 7's
	// `tests/build/prototype-promotion.test.ts` reads it with `readFileSync`, not `import`, so
	// no import graph — static or dynamic — can ever reach it, the same way
	// `type-safety.test-d.ts` above is read by `vue-tsc` rather than imported. Named rather
	// than globbing `tests/fixtures/**`, for the reason `dynamicallyLoaded`'s own comment gives
	// about the concept stylesheets: a glob absorbs the next fixture and tells nobody.
	// `tests/build/fixtures/brokenFake.fixture.ts` and its `vitest.brokenFake.config.ts` are a
	// seventh kind: a deliberately failing spec run by a SPAWNED CHILD vitest, reachable only
	// through that child's own `include` glob. Nothing imports either and no npm script runs
	// them — `tests/build/contractDiscriminates.test.ts` spawns the child and reads its exit
	// code — so fallow's import graph reaches neither and reports both dead. Named rather than
	// globbed `tests/build/fixtures/**`, for the reason `dynamicallyLoaded`'s own comment gives:
	// a glob absorbs the next fixture and tells nobody.
	"entry": [
		"src/main.ts",
		"tests/harness/page.ts",
		"scripts/lint-edited.mjs",
		"scripts/harness-shot.mjs",
		"scripts/concept-shots.mjs",
		"scripts/background-fixture.mjs",
		"tests/application/ports/diagnostics.test-d.ts",
		"tests/presentation/editor/type-safety.test-d.ts",
		"tests/helpers/fixtureVault.test-d.ts",
		"tests/presentation/errors/errorSurfacePolicy.test-d.ts",
		"tests/application/errors/exceptionMapper.test-d.ts",
		"tests/core/money/currency.test-d.ts",
		"tests/domain/cost/costPipelineInput.test-d.ts",
		"tests/presentation/library/assetLibraryContext.test-d.ts",
		"src/prototypes/**/*.vue",
		"tests/fixtures/promotion/ZoneSummary.promoted.vue",
		"tests/build/fixtures/brokenFake.fixture.ts",
		"tests/build/fixtures/vitest.brokenFake.config.ts"
	],
	// Loaded by a page, never imported by a module — which is what this key is for.
	// `docs/user-experience/archive/concepts/*.css` are the concept mocks' sheets: the static HTML
	// files beside them link these, and those files are documentation rather than an entry
	// point, so fallow's import graph cannot reach any of them and reports them dead.
	// Declared as the three files they are rather than as `docs/**/*.css`, so the next
	// stylesheet somebody drops into `docs/` still has to be accounted for instead of being
	// silently covered. That is not a hypothetical: `canvas.css` arrived, this list was not
	// updated, and all four CI legs went red on a change that touched nothing but `docs/`.
	// The paragraph above predicted it exactly, which is the argument FOR the named list —
	// a glob would have absorbed the new file and told nobody.
	"dynamicallyLoaded": [
		"styles/**/*.css",
		"tests/harness/theme.css",
		"tests/harness/obsidian.css",
		"docs/user-experience/archive/concepts/concept.css",
		"docs/user-experience/archive/concepts/page.css",
		"docs/user-experience/archive/concepts/canvas.css"
	],
	// `entry` has no "dev tooling" role the way a framework plugin's `entryPointRole` does —
	// every manual entry is otherwise treated as production-reachable code, so
	// `scripts/harness-shot.mjs`'s own devDependency (`playwright-core`) reads as a
	// devDependency imported by production code, which `dev-dependencies-in-production`
	// exists to catch for a real case: an import a production-only install would break on.
	// This is the opposite — the importing file IS the dev tooling — and fallow's own
	// suggested fix for this exact finding is this key. `@napi-rs/canvas` is the second
	// instance of exactly that shape: `scripts/background-fixture.mjs` draws the smoke
	// test's PNG background with it, and that script is dev tooling declared as an entry.
	//
	// `konva` is `vue-konva`'s PEER dependency, and fallow's advice for it — "test-only
	// production dependency, consider moving to devDependencies" — would be wrong: a peer
	// dependency is the importing package's requirement on ITS host, so this project has to
	// declare it and ship it, while `src/` never names it (the components use `<VStage>` and
	// friends, which `vue-konva` registers). What fallow sees is the test suite importing
	// `konva` directly to inspect the scene it built, which is the only DIRECT import there
	// is. Moving it to devDependencies would produce a plugin that loads in this repository
	// and fails in a vault.
	"ignoreDependencies": ["playwright-core", "@napi-rs/canvas", "konva"],
	"duplicates": {
		"ignore": ["tests/harness/obsidian.css"],
		// The one clone group this repository WANTS: `src/prototypes/ZoneSummary.vue`'s
		// `<template>` block and `tests/fixtures/promotion/ZoneSummary.promoted.vue`'s are
		// byte-identical on purpose — that is the whole criterion
		// `tests/build/prototype-promotion.test.ts` holds, so a clone-detector finding here is
		// the feature working, not copy-paste to refactor away. `ignoredClones` rather than
		// `ignore`, so only this REVIEWED pair is silenced and the two files still get every
		// other duplication check a future edit to either might trip.
		//
		// The second, reviewed with a different verdict and worth the paragraph. ADR-0015's
		// `AssetDesignerView` and slice 5's `PlanEditorView` share twenty lines: the tail of
		// `setState`, the whole of `onOpen` and the whole of `onClose`. Seven statements of it
		// are code and the rest is each file's own comments.
		//
		// Not extracted, and the reason is what the block IS. Six of the seven statements are
		// Obsidian's INTERFACE rather than a rule of this plugin's — `onOpen`/`onClose` are
		// hooks every `ItemView` that mounts a framework tree writes the same way, and a base
		// class holding them would couple two view types for a clone detector rather than for
		// a reason. What could genuinely drift is asserted instead, per view: "both entry
		// points route through one `sync`" has a case in each suite (`mounts … once for state
		// first` / `open first`), "close unmounts" has one in each, and the one line that is a
		// shared rule of ours — `renovation-planner-container`, which `styles/chrome.css` keys
		// off — is now asserted in all THREE view suites, having been asserted in two while
		// the third quietly did without it.
		//
		// The trigger for actually merging them is written down where the decision lives:
		// ADR-0015's *Revisit when* names one view type with a `subject` discriminator, once
		// the designer and the editor want the same shell badly enough that one is less code
		// than two. Tasks B1 and B2 already extracted the halves they really do share — the
		// gesture surface and the tool context.
		//
		// **A clone key is a hash of the clone's own CONTENT, so editing either side retires the
		// suppression** — measured rather than assumed: Task B3a added three members to
		// `AssetDesignerView.mount`'s context literal, the fingerprint moved from `6f87acd9` to
		// `c77b3abb6f87acd9`, and the pair reappeared unhidden having been reviewed once already.
		// Worth knowing before the next edit to either view: a re-keyed group is the same
		// reviewed decision, not a new one, and re-reading the paragraph above is the whole of
		// what it asks for. The key is the id fallow PRINTS plus `:<instance count>`, so
		// `dup:c77b3abb6f87acd9-1` in the report is `dup:c77b3abb6f87acd9-1:2` here — the bare
		// printed id is rejected by the config parser, and a fingerprint without its group index
		// parses and then matches nothing, which is a suppression that hides no group while
		// reading exactly like one that does. Both were measured; the second is the dangerous
		// spelling, because `analyze` still exits 0 for an unhidden duplicate and only the
		// printed report says the key stopped working.
		//
		// The third, and it is a REAL overlap rather than an interface. `createAssetDesignChangeSource`
		// and `createAssetCatalogueChangeSource` share seven lines: the `...EVENTS.map((type) =>
		// events.subscribe(...))` spread and the disposer that walks the list. So do the three
		// other sources in that directory — `planChangeSource`, `projectListChangeSource` and
		// `projectPlansChangeSource` — which is exactly why nothing was extracted for the two the
		// detector happens to pair. A helper adopted by two of five is a third spelling of one
		// mechanism, and half-adoption is how the count of places that disagree becomes
		// unknowable. What the clone spans is MECHANISM and never policy: the event LISTS and the
		// filters differ in every one, and those are the parts a reader has to get right.
		//
		// The trigger: a sixth source, or any change to how a subscription is released — at which
		// point one `subscribeToAll(events, registrations)` in `application/events/` should take
		// ALL FIVE in one edit, and this key should go rather than grow.
		"ignoredClones": ["dup:7fd5d625:2", "dup:c77b3abb6f87acd9-1:2", "dup:c77b3abb6f87acd9-2:2"]
	},
	// The harness page's plugin stylesheet is SERVED, not imported: the dev server answers
	// `/styles.css` from styles/ (scripts/vite-assembled-styles.mjs), so there is no file on
	// disk for a static analyser to resolve — and there must not be one, or the page could
	// show a stale build.
	"ignoreUnresolvedImports": ["/styles.css"],
	// One export, in one file, consumed only through a DYNAMIC import — which fallow's
	// static graph cannot follow, and the dynamic import is not a style choice: `vi.mock` is
	// hoisted above the test file's imports, and the static form fails at runtime with
	// `Cannot access '__vi_import_5__' before initialization` (measured, not assumed). Named
	// rather than globbed, so the next unused export in this helper is still a finding.
	"ignoreExports": [
		{ "file": "tests/helpers/logger.ts", "exports": ["consoleLoggerMock"] }
	],
	// `src/prototypes/**` joins `tests/**` here for a reason that is about THIS FILE rather than
	// about any mock: `vitest.config.ts` excludes that tree from coverage (line 35, "nothing in
	// the tree ships, so measuring it would report on scaffolding"), and fallow's CRAP score is
	// complexity weighted by coverage — so every function in a prototype is scored against 0%
	// and breaches by construction the moment it carries a branch. Measured rather than
	// reasoned: `AssetInspector.vue`'s `shapeNote`, a four-case switch of eight lines, scored
	// CRAP 30.0 against a threshold of 30.
	//
	// It never fired before because no prototype had ever had real logic in it — `<script setup>`
	// only became legal in this tree recently and `WorkPackageFilters.vue`, the first mock to use
	// one, holds a single `ref`. The first mock with a state machine is the first to meet the gap,
	// which is exactly when a config that only ever half-covered a tree gets found.
	//
	// What goes with it, stated rather than smuggled: the SFC template-complexity findings for
	// this tree go too. That is the acceptable half. A mock's template branches BECAUSE it draws
	// states — four mark states, a header with and without a disclosure control, a shelf that is
	// empty — and those states are the entire reason a screen is prototyped before it is built.
	// A mock that shed states to satisfy a complexity metric would be a worse mock, and the
	// metric would have measured it doing its job. `max-lines` (400, ESLint, this tree included)
	// is still the size gate, and it has already forced one decomposition here.
	"health": {
		"coverage": "coverage/coverage-final.json",
		"ignore": ["tests/**", "src/prototypes/**"]
	},
	"rules": {
		"unused-dev-dependencies": "error",
		"unused-optional-dependencies": "error",
		"unused-class-members": "error",
		// Was "warn", which is how nineteen of these accumulated without a gate ever reddening.
		// Cleared to zero first, then ratcheted — the same shape as the coverage floors and the
		// type-check baseline before it: a floor only means something once the tree is under it.
		//
		// Two of the nineteen must NOT be cleared the way the report's first suggested action
		// says, and both carry the reason where the suppression is. `Routed` in
		// `errorSurfacePolicy.ts` is a `unique symbol` its module deliberately never exports —
		// exporting it would let a call site hand-build a toast surface and reach `notifyError`
		// without asking `surfaceFor`. `ReversibleOverrideBase` is internal by construction, and
		// exporting it traded two leaks for an `unused-exports` finding, measured.
		"private-type-leaks": "error"
	},
	// Members a FRAMEWORK invokes — declared rather than suppressed inline, which is what
	// keeps the rule able to report a genuinely dead one. A settings tab's contract is these
	// three: what it declares, what it answers for a control key, and what it does with a new
	// value. Obsidian calls all three; nothing in `src/` does.
	"usedClassMembers": [
		{ "extends": "Plugin", "members": ["onload", "onunload"] },
		{ "extends": "PluginSettingTab", "members": ["getSettingDefinitions", "getControlValue", "setControlValue"] }
	],
	"overrides": [
		{
			"files": ["tests/**"],
			"rules": { "unused-class-members": "off" }
		}
	]
}
